update public.lessons l set enhancements = $ench${"industryStory":"A pattern you see repeatedly in scaling product organisations: a QA team that was perfectly sensible at twenty engineers is quietly overwhelmed at fifty, not because anyone did anything wrong but because the structure was never re-designed as the risk profile changed. Northstar is a textbook case — six testers, centralised and pulled in late, is a shape that made sense when the company was smaller and shipped one product. Now there are four squads on independent cadences, a B2B API being sold against SLAs, and a payments surface handling real money, and the same shape now concentrates every risk decision into a bottleneck at the end of delivery. The team did not degrade; the organisation outgrew a structure nobody revisited. The lesson senior leaders internalise is that team design is not a one-off event at hiring time — it is something you re-open every time the risk profile shifts materially.","visualAid":{"type":"comparison","title":"Centralised vs embedded vs hybrid QA","headers":["Dimension","Centralised","Embedded","Hybrid / matrixed"],"rows":[["Involvement timing","Late — pulled in when code-complete","Early — in the squad from refinement","Early in-squad, with a central standards home"],["Standards consistency","High — one bar for all squads","Low — drifts per squad","Medium-high — guild sets the bar"],["Specialist skills","Easier to protect and pool","Diluted; one tester can't be all things","Pooled centrally, pulled by squads"],["Ownership of quality","Risk of \"QA owns quality\"","Shared with the squad","Shared, with a professional backbone"],["Main failure mode","Bottleneck at the end","Isolation and quiet deprioritisation","Matrix tension — two masters"],["Fits Northstar when","Never again at this scale","Only where risk is squad-local","The pragmatic target for most scaling orgs"]]},"davidTip":"The question worth asking every candidate for a lead role is not \"which model do you prefer?\" — it is \"how would you decide?\" Anyone can recite the pros and cons of embedded versus centralised; that is table stakes. What separates a senior leader is starting from risk and budget and being willing to say out loud, \"we are not covering X well this quarter, and here is why that is the right call.\" Executives trust a leader who names their own gaps far more than one who claims full coverage on a shoestring — the second one is either naive or lying, and Priya can smell both. Design the team you can actually staff, defend it in risk terms, and revisit it the moment the risk profile moves.","badGood":{"label":"proposing a team structure to a VP","bad":"\"We should embed a tester in every squad and build a QA centre of excellence — it's industry best practice and it's how the big companies do it.\" — structure-first, copied, no risk argument, and unstaffable with six people.","good":"\"Our top three risks are payments, the B2B API and web accessibility. I'm putting our two strongest testers where risk and lateness bite hardest, keeping a thin central function for standards and the automation problem, and covering performance and accessibility as pull-on capabilities. I'm deliberately going light on Mobile this quarter — that's a conscious risk I'll re-open in Q3.\" — risk-first, staffable, names its own gaps."},"miniChallenge":"Your CTO returns from a conference convinced Northstar should adopt a specific big-tech \"squads, chapters and guilds\" model wholesale, and asks you to roll it out next quarter. You think the underlying ideas are sound but a literal copy is wrong for a 55-person org with six testers. What do you actually say in that meeting, and what do you propose instead?","modelAnswer":"## Example\nI would agree with the CTO's underlying goal — early involvement plus consistent standards — before touching the mechanism, because the worst outcome is a turf fight that reads as QA resisting change. Then I would separate the idea from the artefact: \"The chapters-and-guilds thinking is exactly right for us; a literal copy of a company thirty times our size is not, because they have a specialist per squad and we have six people total.\" I would propose a hybrid we can actually staff: embed our two strongest testers where risk is highest (payments and API), run a lightweight QA guild for standards, the skills matrix and career, and treat performance/security/accessibility as central pull-on capabilities rather than per-squad roles. I would frame the whole thing against our escaped-defect and change-failure data so the CTO sees it as delivering his goal, not blocking it — and I would commit to a review point so it is explicitly a first iteration, not a permanent monument.","portfolioBuilder":"This lesson opens your module portfolio deliverable, the **QA Capability Development Plan**. Draft its first two sections now: (1) an **organisational context and risk profile** for Northstar — its products, delivery model, top five quality risks ranked with rough cost-of-failure, and constraints (Series B, cost-scrutinised headcount); and (2) a **target operating model** — the team shape you are proposing, what is embedded, what is central, and the risks you are consciously covering lightly. Every later section (skills matrix, gap analysis, development plans, progression) will trace back to this risk profile, so make it concrete and honest.","resourcePreview":{"name":"QA Team Design Canvas","purpose":"A one-page template that forces you from risk profile to team structure in the right order — risks first, headcount and model last.","whenToUse":"Use it at the start of the module assignment, and any time you inherit or re-design a real QA function.","formats":["PDF","Editable template"]}}$ench$::jsonb
