update public.lessons l set enhancements = $ench${"industryStory":"A recurring trap in QA hiring is the \"role that hides a skill gap\". An organisation feels its automation is weak, so it opens a requisition for an automation engineer — a role — because a role is easy to budget and easy to explain. What the situation actually needed was often quite different: coaching the existing testers, redesigning a suite that was structurally flaky, or getting developers to own their own tests. The new hire arrives, inherits the same broken suite and the same isolation the previous owner had, and eighteen months later the organisation opens the same requisition again, having learned nothing. Northstar is one requisition away from exactly this: \"hire two SDETs to fix automation\" would very likely reproduce Dan's situation at twice the cost. The senior move is to refuse to convert a skill-and-process problem into a headcount problem without first proving that a hire is genuinely the cheapest fix.","visualAid":{"type":"comparison","title":"Skill, the risk it reduces, and how Northstar should acquire it","headers":["Skill","Risk it reduces","Best acquisition for Northstar"],"rows":[["Exploratory testing","Green pipeline, unhappy users","Core in-house strength — protect and grow it"],["UI/E2E automation","Slow, costly manual regression","Fix reliability first; develop, don't just hire"],["API/service automation","Regression at integration seams; SLA confidence","Develop across the team; underpins B2B SLAs"],["SDET / test infrastructure","Testing that doesn't scale with the code","One skilled hire or develop Dan — not two bolt-ons"],["Performance testing","System falls over under real load","Raise awareness + buy/borrow when it spikes"],["Accessibility","Legal, reputational, inclusion exposure (web)","Baseline awareness in-house + periodic specialist audit"],["Security-aware testing","Shipping obvious vulnerabilities","Awareness across generalists; partner with security"],["Domain knowledge (payments)","Testing the wrong thing correctly","Grow in-house; it cannot be contracted in"]]},"davidTip":"Watch your own language in planning meetings. The moment you hear yourself — or an executive — say \"we need to hire a [role]\", treat it as a prompt to stop and translate: which risk, which skills, and is hiring really the cheapest route? More budget gets wasted on filling roles than on almost any technical decision, because a role is concrete and comforting and a skill gap is fuzzy and uncomfortable. Consider an organisation that opens a requisition for a senior automation engineer to \"fix the flaky pipeline\", recruits a perfectly capable one after three months, and finds a year later that the suite is still slow and distrusted — because the real problem was test design, developer ownership and a suite nobody had time to re-architect, none of which a fresh pair of hands inherits its way out of. The salary bought motion, not the fix; a few weeks of coaching and a deliberate redesign would have moved the risk the hire never touched. It is a common pattern, and it is expensive precisely because a role feels like decisive action while a skill gap feels like an admission. The discipline of translating roles into skills is unglamorous and it will save you a headcount every year.","badGood":{"label":"responding to \"good developers don't need a QA team\" (Marcus)","bad":"\"Every serious engineering org has a QA team — we add a layer of quality assurance and a second pair of eyes before release.\" — defends the role and the headcount, invites exactly the \"layer / gate\" objection Marcus already holds.","good":"\"Agreed that responsibility for quality can't be outsourced to a team. What we bring is specific capability: exploratory depth on the payment edge cases your unit tests won't reach, and ownership of the regression signal you rely on to ship confidently. That's a skills-and-risk question, not a headcount one.\" — argues capability against risk, which a sceptic can actually engage with."},"miniChallenge":"Your budget allows exactly one new QA hire next year. The web squad wants a dedicated accessibility specialist (there is real legal exposure), the API squad wants an SDET to build service-level automation for the B2B SLAs, and the payments squad quietly needs deeper domain expertise. You cannot hire three people or seriously develop all three skills at once. Which single skill do you acquire by hiring, and how do you cover the other two without a hire?","modelAnswer":"## Example\nI would spend the one hire on the SDET / service-automation skill, because it reduces the B2B SLA risk the business is actively trying to sell against, and — unlike the other two — it is a capability that scales the whole team's testing, not just one risk area. Accessibility I would cover by raising baseline WCAG awareness across the web testers (training plus tooling in CI) and buying a specialist audit a couple of times a year, since the demand is periodic rather than continuous and does not justify a full-time role. Payments domain depth cannot be contracted in and must not be siloed, so I would grow it in-house by pairing a strong tester with Marcus's squad over time to absorb the domain, which also starts to thaw his scepticism. The reasoning I would present to the VP is explicitly acquisition-by-risk: hire where the skill both reduces the highest-value risk and scales, develop where the skill must be retained (domain), and buy where the demand is spiky (accessibility).","resourcePreview":{"name":"Skills-not-Roles Translation Sheet","purpose":"A quick reference that maps common QA job titles to the underlying skills and the risks they reduce, so you can challenge a \"hire a role\" request in real time.","whenToUse":"Keep it to hand in workforce-planning and budget conversations.","formats":["PDF"]}}$ench$::jsonb
