update public.lessons l set enhancements = $ench${"industryStory":"The most common failure mode for a skills matrix is not a bad matrix — it is a good matrix that dies in a folder. A team runs an away-day, produces a colourful grid, everyone nods, and it is never opened again because it was built as an event rather than an instrument. The matrices that earn their keep are the ones referenced in ordinary decisions: who to put on which squad, whether it is safe to let someone take three weeks' leave, what to say when Finance questions a training line. Northstar's team has never had one at all, which is precisely why capability arguments there are currently lost on gut feel — Sofia's leadership is invisible, Dan's key-person risk is unquantified, and the missing specialisms are a vague unease rather than a funded plan. The matrix does not create those facts; it makes them arguable, and \"arguable\" is what unlocks budget.","visualAid":{"type":"comparison","title":"Northstar QA skills matrix (0=None 1=Aware 2=Working 3=Proficient 4=Leading)","headers":["Competency","Sofia","Dan","Raj","Ben","Chloe","Aisha","Team read"],"rows":[["Exploratory testing","4","2","3","3","2","1","Strong bench"],["Test analysis & design","3","2","3","2","2","1","Healthy"],["UI/E2E automation","1","3","1","0","2","0","Bus factor 1 (Dan)"],["API/service automation","1","2","1","1","1","0","Thin — SLA risk"],["CI/CD & test infra","0","3","0","0","1","0","Bus factor 1 (Dan)"],["Performance testing","1","1","0","0","0","0","GAP — no capability"],["Security-aware testing","1","1","1","0","0","0","GAP — no capability"],["Accessibility testing","1","0","1","0","1","0","GAP — legal exposure"],["Domain knowledge (payments)","2","1","3","2","1","0","Concentrated in Raj"],["Risk-based strategy","3","1","2","2","1","0","Rests on Sofia"],["Stakeholder influence","3","1","2","1","1","1","Rests on Sofia"],["Coaching & mentoring","3","1","2","1","1","0","Sofia leads unrecognised"]]},"davidTip":"Two pieces of hard-won advice on matrices. First, never let the numbers near pay or a ranking exercise — the instant people believe a low score costs them money, every cell drifts to 3 and your evidence base is worthless. Keep it explicitly developmental and say so out loud. Second, resist the urge to build it alone and present it finished; the score is the cheap part, the calibration conversation is where you learn that Dan is drowning and Sofia is running the team in all but name. When you present a matrix to an executive, never show the whole grid — a twelve-by-six sea of numbers makes eyes glaze. Show three conclusions with three price tags. The grid is your working; the conclusions are the argument.","badGood":{"label":"presenting team capability to a VP","bad":"\"Here's our skills matrix — as you can see the team scores well on most competencies and we've identified some areas for development.\" — shows the grid, buries the risk, gives the VP nothing to decide or fund.","good":"\"Three findings. One: we have zero real capability against performance, security and accessibility — three risks tied to our SLAs and legal exposure. Two: our entire automation and CI capability is one person who's near burnout. Three: our senior tester is leading the team with no title or support. Here's the plan and what each costs.\" — conclusions and prices, decision-ready."},"miniChallenge":"Using Northstar's matrix, you have budget to raise exactly one competency by one level for the whole team this quarter (through training and pairing), OR to eliminate one bus-factor-of-one by developing a second person to level 2. You cannot do both. Which do you choose, and what is the risk you are explicitly leaving open by not choosing the other?","modelAnswer":"## Example\nI would spend the quarter eliminating the automation/CI bus factor by developing a second person — Chloe, given her 2 in test design and stated curiosity — toward level 2 in UI/E2E automation and basic CI, paired with Dan. My reasoning is that a coverage gap (say, accessibility at top-score 1) is a risk that materialises gradually and can be partly mitigated by buying an external audit, whereas the bus factor is a risk that materialises catastrophically and instantly the day Dan resigns or burns out — and he owns the very signal the whole company relies on to ship. Reducing a low-probability-but-catastrophic single point of failure beats nudging a whole competency up one notch. The risk I am explicitly leaving open is that we remain at zero real capability on performance, security and accessibility for another quarter; I would name that to Priya, propose covering accessibility with a bought audit in the interim, and put the specialist-capability build on next quarter's plan so it is a deferred decision, not a forgotten one.","portfolioBuilder":"The skills matrix is the analytical heart of your **QA Capability Development Plan**. Add to your portfolio: (1) the full completed Northstar matrix with your competencies, your anchored proficiency scale, and every cell scored with a one-line rationale for the borderline calls; and (2) a short \"how to read this\" note stating the coverage gaps, key-person risks and bench strengths you draw from it. This section is what every other part of the plan — the gap analysis, the development plans, the progression ladder — will reference, so make the competencies and anchors genuinely defensible.","resourcePreview":{"name":"QA Competency & Proficiency Framework","purpose":"A starter competency library plus a behaviourally-anchored 0–4 proficiency scale you can adapt to your own risk profile, so you are not defining levels from a blank page.","whenToUse":"Use it when building your first matrix; prune and re-word the competencies to fit your organisation rather than adopting them wholesale.","formats":["PDF","Editable template"]}}$ench$::jsonb
