update public.lessons l set enhancements = $ench${"industryStory":"The tell-tale sign of an immature capability plan is that every gap resolves to \"hire someone\". It is a comfortable answer — a requisition is concrete and it feels like decisive action — but it collides immediately with a Series-B reality where, as at Northstar, a request for three testers gets challenged and only a request framed as risk reduction with ROI gets a hearing. The leaders who get funded are the ones who show a mixed strategy: build the retained skills, buy the one genuinely permanent gap, borrow the spiky ones, and stand up in front of the VP and say \"we are consciously not covering this yet, here is the risk we are carrying, and here is the trigger to revisit it.\" That sentence feels dangerous the first time you say it — it sounds like admitting failure. It is the opposite: it is the single most credible thing a capability plan can contain, because it proves you have made choices rather than written a wish list, and it gives the executive a real decision to own alongside you.","visualAid":{"type":"comparison","title":"Choosing a gap-closure strategy","headers":["Strategy","Best when","Speed / cost","Watch out for","Northstar example"],"rows":[["Build (develop)","Skill must be retained & used continuously","Slow / moderate; compounds","Too slow for an urgent, sudden risk","Second automation person; payments domain"],["Buy (hire)","Permanent continuous need at a level you can't grow fast","Slow to recruit / high; scrutinised","Filling a role that was really a process gap","One senior SDET to anchor infrastructure"],["Borrow (contract/partner)","Demand is spiky or you need to bootstrap","Fast / flexible; builds no lasting capability","Using it to cover a key-person risk","Performance bursts; accessibility audits; security partnering"],["Accept (carry the risk)","Gap is low priority this period","Free now; deferred risk","Forgetting to log it and set a revisit trigger","No in-house accessibility depth this quarter, logged"]]},"davidTip":"The one word to add to your vocabulary this lesson is \"accept\". Junior leaders think their job is to close every gap; senior leaders know their job is to close the *right* gaps and to consciously, visibly carry the rest. When you review a capability plan, the first thing to look for is the accepted-risk section — if there isn't one, don't trust the rest of the document, because nobody on a real budget can cover everything and a plan that claims to is either dishonest or naive. Write the accepted risks down, put a trigger against each (\"revisit if a partner SLA breach occurs\", \"revisit if regulatory exposure on the web app increases\"), and review them on a cadence. That discipline is what turns an ignored gap — which is negligence — into an accepted risk, which is a decision your executive co-owns.","badGood":{"label":"a capability gap analysis line for accessibility","bad":"\"Accessibility: current capability low. Action: hire an accessibility specialist.\" — defaults to buy, ignores that demand is periodic, and would be challenged instantly on a cost-scrutinised budget.","good":"\"Accessibility: legal/reputational exposure on the consumer web app; current top score 1. Priority medium (lower immediate likelihood than the automation bus factor). Strategy: borrow — external WCAG audit twice yearly (~£X) plus build baseline awareness in the web testers via training in CI. Accepting: no in-house accessibility depth this quarter; trigger to revisit — any accessibility complaint or a rise in regulatory exposure.\" — classified, prioritised, mixed strategy, risk named."},"miniChallenge":"Priya approves budget to fully close exactly one of Northstar's gaps this quarter and tells you the rest must be borrowed or accepted. The candidates are: (a) the automation/CI bus factor, (b) the SLA-linked API depth gap, (c) the accessibility coverage gap with its legal exposure. Which one do you spend the \"fully close\" budget on, and how do you handle the other two so that neither becomes negligence?","modelAnswer":"## Example\nI would spend the full-close budget on the automation/CI bus factor (a), because it is the only one of the three that is simultaneously high-likelihood, high-cost and catastrophic-if-sudden — losing Dan removes the entire regression signal overnight, whereas the other two degrade gradually and have interim mitigations. I would build a second internal person to level 2 and stabilise the suite, because this is a retained-skill key-person risk that only building can close. For the API depth gap (b) I would borrow to bootstrap — a short senior-SDET contract or consulting engagement to establish the service-level framework — while pairing the team on it so capability starts transferring in-house, explicitly noting this only *starts* closing an SLA-linked risk and flagging it as next quarter's build priority. For accessibility (c) I would borrow a periodic external audit and accept the in-house depth gap, but I would make that acceptance explicit and logged with a clear trigger (any complaint or increased regulatory exposure) so it is a co-owned decision rather than a quietly ignored liability. The shape of the answer is the point: one build, one borrow-to-bootstrap, one logged-accept — a plan a Series-B VP can actually fund and defend.","portfolioBuilder":"Add the **gap analysis** to your QA Capability Development Plan: a single prioritised page listing each gap from your matrix, its type (coverage / key-person / depth), its priority (likelihood × cost), its closure strategy (build / buy / borrow / accept), a rough cost, and — for every accepted gap — the named risk and its revisit trigger. This section is the bridge between the matrix (what is) and the development plans and progression ladder (what you will do about it), so make the prioritisation and the accepted-risk logic explicit and defensible.","resourcePreview":{"name":"Build-Buy-Borrow-Accept Decision Aid","purpose":"A short decision aid that walks a single gap through classification, prioritisation and closure-strategy selection, including how to write up an accepted risk.","whenToUse":"Use it per gap when producing a capability gap analysis or defending one to finance.","formats":["PDF"]}}$ench$::jsonb
