update public.lessons l set enhancements = $ench${"industryStory":"The most expensive development mistake is confusing motion with growth. An organisation notices a tester is \"always busy on automation\" and concludes they are developing; in reality they are trapped in a maintenance treadmill that is teaching them nothing new and slowly burning them out — which is Dan's situation exactly. Genuine development almost always requires *taking something off* someone as well as adding something: you cannot grow a person you keep at 110% utilisation, because growth needs the slack to attempt something unfamiliar and get it wrong safely. Picture a QA function where the strongest automation engineer is buried in the daily firefighting of a brittle suite — visibly the busiest person on the team and, by any honest reading, learning nothing new. Time and again the move that changes such a person's trajectory is subtractive first: hand part of the maintenance load to a second engineer paired alongside them, and only then offer a genuine stretch — ownership of a re-architecture, or the design of a faster, more reliable framework — with a safety net of regular support so the unfamiliar work can go wrong without blame. Organisations that do this tend to find that the same engineer who looked burnt out and static six months earlier is now defining the approach others follow and coaching the person who took the load off them. The load came off before the growth went on, and that order is the whole point. The leaders who grow strong teams treat capacity as the raw material of development, not just of delivery.","visualAid":{"type":"comparison","title":"Development mechanisms and when to reach for each","headers":["Mechanism","Roughly","Best for","Northstar use"],"rows":[["Stretch assignment","On-the-job (the ~70%)","Building durable, applied skill with a safety net","Chloe co-owning part of the automation suite"],["Pairing / shadowing","Learning from others (the ~20%)","Fast transfer of tacit skill; breaking isolation","Aisha with a strong exploratory tester; Chloe with Dan"],["Coaching","Learning from others (the ~20%)","Growing judgement and leadership, not just technique","Sofia's growth into recognised leadership"],["Community of practice","Learning from others (the ~20%)","Shared standards; defeating embedded isolation","A QA guild across the four squads"],["Formal training / cert","Formal (the ~10%)","Bootstrapping vocabulary and fundamentals fast","A CI/architecture course to support the suite redesign"]]},"davidTip":"If you take one habit from this lesson, make it this: before you spend a penny of training budget, ask what piece of real work plus what support would teach the skill better. The training industry is very good at selling courses; almost nothing durable that people learn comes primarily from a classroom, and almost everything comes from being handed something slightly too hard with someone in their corner. The corollary that leaders forget is that development needs slack — you cannot grow someone you are running at full utilisation, which is why \"develop Dan\" and \"stop overloading Dan\" are the same instruction. And on the Sofias of the world: when a good senior is wary because of a past failure, that wariness is data, not defiance. Bring them into designing the change rather than announcing it to them, and you convert your biggest sceptic into your strongest ally.","badGood":{"label":"a development plan for a burning-out lone automation engineer","bad":"\"Dan is our automation expert, so we'll fund an advanced automation certification to deepen his expertise and keep him engaged.\" — adds load to an overloaded person, develops no second holder, closes no key-person risk, and misreads burnout as an engagement problem a course can fix.","good":"\"We'll pair Chloe with Dan to co-own the suite over two quarters — relieving his load, transferring his knowledge to close our worst bus factor, and repositioning Dan from lone maintainer toward test-architecture and coaching, with a coaching cadence on that shift and one targeted course to support the redesign.\" — one move that serves the person's growth, the team's risk and retention together."},"miniChallenge":"Sofia agrees, cautiously, to lead a fresh look at Northstar's automation strategy — the very area where last year's \"automate everything\" push failed and burned the team. She is clearly still braced for it to go the same way. What are the first three concrete things you do to make this a development opportunity that rebuilds her trust rather than a repeat that confirms her fear?","modelAnswer":"## Example\nFirst, I would give her genuine decision authority, not just delivery responsibility — she owns the strategy's direction and success criteria, and I say so publicly to the squads and to Priya, because last time the direction was imposed and she was left to make a doomed approach work. Second, I would explicitly define what \"success\" means this time in modest, falsifiable terms (a stabilised subset of the suite, a trusted signal, not \"automate everything\"), so the goal itself is different from the one that failed and she is not being set up to repeat an impossible brief. Third, I would give her air cover and a safety net: agreed check-ins where she can raise problems without it reading as failure, my visible backing when she pushes back on unrealistic expectations from Tom or the CTO, and a clear message that if the strategy needs to change course that is a sign of good judgement, not of the initiative failing. The through-line is that her wariness is earned information about how the organisation behaved last time, so the development work is as much about changing the organisation's behaviour toward her as about growing her leadership skills — do the first and the second follows.","portfolioBuilder":"Add the **individual development plans** to your QA Capability Development Plan — at minimum for Sofia, Dan and one other Northstar tester, ideally the full team. Each IDP should show the team capability gap it helps close (tying back to your gap analysis), the individual's motivation, concrete experience-weighted actions (stretch, pairing, coaching, with formal training only where it fits), and a check-in cadence. Make explicit how the Dan plan doubles as your key-person-risk closure action and how the Sofia plan rebuilds trust before adding expectations — those two are where a senior reviewer will look hardest.","resourcePreview":{"name":"Individual Development Plan Template (QA)","purpose":"A one-page IDP template built around the gap-plus-motivation intersection and experience-weighted actions, with prompts for check-in cadence and success signals.","whenToUse":"Use it per team member during development planning and revisit it at each check-in rather than annually.","formats":["PDF","Editable template"]}}$ench$::jsonb
from public.modules m join public.courses c on c.id = m.course_id
where l.module_id = m.id and c.slug = 'qa-leadership-academy'
  and m.module_number = 3 and l.lesson_number = 5;

update public.lessons l set enhancements = $ench${"industryStory":"The most damaging progression failure is the invisible one: there is no ladder, so nothing appears to be wrong until a strong tester resigns \"for growth\" and the exit interview reveals they could not see a future and never raised it because there was nothing concrete to raise it against. That is Northstar's live exposure — a good team with no framework, an informal senior (Sofia) leading without title or recognition, and a specialist (Dan) with nowhere to grow but sideways into more of the same firefighting. The absence of a ladder reads to staff as an absence of a future, and people do not usually announce that conclusion; they just start interviewing. What makes the fix hard is not the design — competency levels and a dual track are well-understood — it is the honesty a real ladder demands: admitting how few senior slots a 55-person company has, refusing to inflate titles under retention pressure, and paying the IC track genuinely equally. The organisations that get this right treat the ladder as a promise they can actually keep, which is why a modest, honest framework beats an elaborate one the company can never fill.","visualAid":{"type":"comparison","title":"A dual-ladder QA progression framework (competency & scope based)","headers":["Level","Shared / Track","Demonstrated capability","Scope of impact","Northstar fit"],"rows":[["Junior QA","Shared","Works on defined tasks with support","Own tasks, guided","Aisha (ramping hire)"],["QA Engineer","Shared","Independent on own work; routine competencies at 2","Own work","Chloe, developing"],["Senior QA","Shared (fork point)","Owns quality for an area; sets local standards; mentors","A squad / area","Raj, Ben; Sofia today"],["Principal / Staff QA","Technical (IC) track","Defines testing approach & architecture; org-wide technical authority","The whole function, technically","Dan's growth path"],["QA Manager / Head of QA","Management track","Leads people, budget and QA strategy","The whole function, organisationally","Sofia's deliberate path"],["(Equal pay/status?)","Both tracks","IC and management tracks must be genuinely comparable","Or the \"dual\" ladder is a fiction","Non-negotiable design rule"]]},"davidTip":"Two things sink career frameworks, and both are about honesty rather than design. The first is the fake dual ladder: an organisation proudly announces a technical track that quietly tops out a pay band below the management track, and every senior IC does the arithmetic in about thirty seconds and heads for management anyway. If you cannot pay the top of the IC track the same as the equivalent manager, do not claim it is dual — you will lose the trust the framework was supposed to build. The second is over-promising headroom in a small company; it is far better to tell a strong tester \"there is realistically one Principal slot here and here is honestly where you stand for it, and here is how we grow your scope meanwhile\" than to dangle a promotion the org chart cannot deliver and watch their trust curdle when it never comes. A ladder is a promise. Only build the rungs you can actually stand on, and your credibility as a leader will outlast any glossy framework.","badGood":{"label":"pitching a QA progression framework to the exec team","bad":"\"Our QA team wants career progression and titles, so I've designed a nine-level framework with senior, staff, principal and distinguished grades to give everyone room to grow.\" — framed as staff wants, over-engineered for 55 people, promises headroom that doesn't exist, invites level inflation.","good":"\"We have no QA ladder, which is a churn and recruitment risk given our cost and retention goals. I'm proposing a lean competency-based framework in the language we already assess against, with a genuine dual IC/management track at senior level so we keep strong testers like Dan without forcing them into management — and I'm being explicit that at our size there are only one or two senior slots, so we'll grow scope laterally where vertical slots are scarce.\" — framed as retention and fairness, sized to the org, honest about headroom."},"miniChallenge":"You roll out the dual ladder. Within a month, three testers separately ask to be promoted to the new \"Senior QA\" level, and the CTO — worried about a competitor poaching them — quietly suggests you just grant the titles to keep them happy, even though the matrix shows only one is clearly operating at that level. What do you do, and how do you say no to the CTO without appearing to block retention?","modelAnswer":"## Example\nI would not grant titles the competency evidence does not support, and I would tell the CTO exactly why in his own terms: inflating the level to retain three people devalues \"Senior\" for everyone, creates a pay and credibility problem we will pay for later, and — critically — makes the framework instantly untrustworthy to the very people it is meant to reassure, because they will see it hands out titles for retention rather than demonstrated capability. I would reframe his retention worry rather than dismiss it: for the one tester clearly operating at senior level, I promote now with the matrix evidence to show it is earned. For the other two, I show them precisely what they are already demonstrating and the one or two specific competencies still to evidence, with a concrete development plan and a realistic timeframe — which is far more motivating and retentive than a hollow title, because it is a credible path rather than a gift. To the CTO I would put it as: \"The fastest way to lose all three is a ladder they can see is fake; the way to keep them is a ladder they can see is real, one of them promoted on merit now and the other two with a clear, evidenced path.\" That protects the framework's integrity and serves his retention goal at the same time — and if headroom genuinely constrains the promotions, I say that openly too rather than let the ladder quietly jam.","managersReview":{"intro":"When your completed QA Capability Development Plan is reviewed by your lead — the way a real one would be scrutinised before it went to an executive like Priya — here is what that review looks for and where these plans typically fall short:","strengths":["The team structure and every capability decision trace explicitly back to a named risk profile, not to a generic template","The skills matrix uses behaviourally-anchored levels and its conclusions (coverage gaps, key-person risk, bench strength) are drawn out clearly","The plan uses a genuine mix of build/buy/borrow/accept and names the risks it is consciously carrying rather than pretending to cover everything","Development is weighted toward on-the-job experience and pairing, and the Dan plan doubles as the key-person-risk closure"],"improvements":["Tie every development and progression cost to risk reduction and retention when presenting upward — executives fund those, not \"growth\"","Keep the artefacts short and decision-ready; lead with three conclusions and prices, not a twelve-by-six grid","Make the dual-ladder pay-parity and the headroom reality explicit — a reviewer will look there first for honesty"],"gaps":["Defaulting gaps to \"hire\" and under-using build/borrow/accept on a cost-scrutinised Series-B budget","A skills matrix that is assessed once and never revisited, so it cannot show whether development worked","A dual ladder that is dual on paper but unequal on pay, or a framework promising headroom a 55-person org cannot deliver","Steamrolling Sofia's earned wariness instead of designing the change with her"]},"portfolioBuilder":"This lesson completes your module deliverable: the full **QA Capability Development Plan** for Northstar. Assemble the sections you have built across the module into one coherent document: (1) organisational context and risk profile plus target operating model (Lesson 1); (2) the completed skills matrix with anchored levels and its risk conclusions (Lesson 3); (3) the prioritised gap analysis with build/buy/borrow/accept strategies and logged accepted risks (Lesson 4); (4) individual development plans, at least for Sofia and Dan (Lesson 5); and (5) this progression framework with its dual ladder and honest headroom paragraph. Add a one-page executive summary written for Priya — three headline risks, the plan to address them, the cost, and the risks you are asking the business to consciously carry. That executive summary is the single most important page; write it last, and write it in risk-and-cost language, not QA jargon.","resourcePreview":{"name":"QA Progression Framework Blueprint","purpose":"A lean, competency-based dual-ladder blueprint (levels, scope-of-impact descriptors and a dual-track pay-parity checklist) that you can adapt to your organisation's size and headroom rather than designing from scratch.","whenToUse":"Use it to draft or audit a real QA career framework, and as the model for the progression section of your Capability Development Plan.","formats":["PDF","Editable template"]}}$ench$::jsonb
from public.modules m join public.courses c on c.id = m.course_id
where l.module_id = m.id and c.slug = 'qa-leadership-academy'
  and m.module_number = 3 and l.lesson_number = 6;

